---
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Career from '../components/Career.astro';
import { FAVORITED } from '../data/past-work';

// TODO(Elliott): add your portrait at public/images/elliott.jpg (square, at least 640×640). Until it exists the page
// renders the "E" monogram instead; the check runs at build time, so rebuild after adding it.
const hasPortrait = fs.existsSync(path.join(process.cwd(), 'public/images/elliott.jpg'));

const profiles = [
  { href: 'https://github.com/elliott-io', label: 'GitHub', handle: 'elliott-io', icon: 'github' },
  { href: 'https://github.com/elliott-wave-labs', label: 'GitHub organisation', handle: 'elliott-wave-labs', icon: 'github' },
  { href: 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/elliottio/', label: 'LinkedIn', handle: 'elliottio', icon: 'linkedin' },
  { href: 'https://www.instagram.com/khensu.nu', label: 'Instagram', handle: 'khensu.nu', icon: 'instagram' },
];
---

<Base title="About" description="elliottwave.studio lab makes technology and art, and blurs the line between the two. Founded by Elliott. Creators of MOQOM, six10nine, Unify and Kalopsia.">
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>elliottwave.studio lab</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        An independent technology lab and studio. We create technology in categories of its own, starting with MOQOM.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap two">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The company</p>
        <h2>A lab, and the owner of its work.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          elliottwave.studio lab is the brand of Elliott Wave LLC, the company behind MOQOM, six10nine, Unify and Kalopsia.
        </p>
        <p>
          Elliott Wave LLC, founded and solely owned by Elliott, owns all intellectual property in its projects — the
          open-source projects included: the code, the designs, the trademarks, and the inventions. An open-source licence
          lets you use the code; it does not transfer ownership. Patents pending.
        </p>
        {/* TODO(Elliott): optional, a line on why the studio is called Elliott Wave. */}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap two">
      <div class="founder-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Founder</p>
        {hasPortrait ? (
          <img class="portrait" src="/images/elliott.jpg" alt="Justin Elliott Staffer in Tangier, Morocco" width="240" height="240" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        ) : (
          <div class="portrait monogram" role="img" aria-label="Elliott">E</div>
        )}
        <h2 class="founder-name">About Elliott <span class="full-name">Justin Elliott Staffer</span></h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p><strong>Elliott — Founder of elliottwave.studio lab and Elliott Wave LLC.</strong></p>
        <p>Founder and sole engineer, building technology in categories of its own. Elliott is the sole author of the studio’s first-party technology, including MOQOM, six10nine, Unify and Kalopsia.</p>
        <p>
          His newest project, <a href="/khensu">khensu.nu</a>, a post-quantum private messenger, was inspired by Pavel,
          Telegram’s founder, for its style, and by Moxie Marlinspike, for the security of Signal. Moxie’s Signal
          Protocol was the best in class when WhatsApp adopted it, and Brian, WhatsApp’s co-founder, later sounded the
          alarm over what was happening to WhatsApp, left, and co-founded the Signal Foundation with Moxie.
          khensu.nu is built to supersede that work: post-quantum end to end, signatures included, with every message
          carried through a mixnet so no one can see who talks to whom, on a network run by its people. With credit and
          thanks to Moxie, Pavel and Brian. khensu.nu is not affiliated with Telegram, Signal or WhatsApp.
        </p>
        <p>
          Avidly product- and UX-minded, he builds security, AI and quality in from the start, with compliance designed
          in from day one: SOC 2 (all five trust criteria), ISO 27001, GDPR, CCPA/CPRA and data subject access requests
          (controls mapped; not yet audited or certified). He has written his own encryption
          and archival platform, built for clients including Disney.
        </p>
        <p>
          He also wrote the visual search that won Disney’s archive contract and kept it for years. It covered all of
          their archive data, new and old, including the original Star Wars footage, with full DNxHD downloads over
          internal, secured networks.
        </p>
        <p>Born in Toronto and raised in Los Angeles from a toddler, he loves travel and connecting people, in the hope of making the world a better place where everyone wins and has fun doing it. He loves learning new things, and mentoring others while he does. When he isn’t building, he cooks.</p>
        <p>
          Elliott is also a Member of Technical Staff at <a href={FAVORITED.site} rel="noopener">Favorited</a>, where the
          team just launched the native apps with{' '}
          <a href={FAVORITED.event} rel="noopener">Spooky Season 2026</a> on the App Store: hunt ghosts live, find the amulet.
          He spearheaded Favorited’s international live captions and translations: <strong>streamers can go live in
          their own language and reach audiences across the world</strong>, and viewers follow along in theirs as it
          happens. As far as we know, Favorited is the only livestream app that has it. Favorited’s full plans are
          unfolding rapidly, with incredible results from the entire team.
          His work there includes several new technologies and improvements on existing ones, now in an exploratory patent
          phase; that work belongs to Favorited, not the lab. The MOQOM work was done about a month ago, but Favorited’s
          launch came first, so its release waited until now.
        </p>
        <p>
          Right before Favorited, he was CTO of LIVEr / Aioli, where he built a RAG engine and custom agents working with 50,000 restaurants
          across America, with live updates, on Google’s APIs and other publicly available data. He got Aioli into Google’s
          startup program for AI companies. As a hands-on CTO he led a team of ten developers through Aioli’s full Agora
          implementation. Favorited, where he works now, is the second company where he has worked with Agora. Aioli also livestreamed, so restaurants and shops could sell food on air: the full
          food ordering system, with agents that learned from each customer and made custom recommendations, was working, tested and launched before he left, and shops were partly built. Favorited
          recruited him away as Aioli’s funding ran low. He started there in April 2025, a birthday present to himself,
          trading the Bay Area tech scene for Los Angeles, where he was raised, and Silicon Beach, his favourite.
        </p>
        <p>
          In 2024 he also started <a href="https://getvarasa.com" rel="noopener">Varasa</a>, human-centred AI, with Sonia
          Talati. He is its founder and sole creator, with patents on private AI workflows.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before that, as lead iOS engineer at Banter Media, he built the same idea for messages you answer later: video,
          audio, text and documents, transcribed and translated automatically, with groups, AI summaries and AI
          functions. He built it while living in Morocco for nearly two years. Banter ran out of funding when Silicon Valley Bank collapsed and
          couldn’t raise again. Then came travel: a captain’s sailing licence in Cartagena, Colombia, and time exploring
          São Paulo. The sailing bug goes back to Greece in 2015: two weeks sailing between the islands, and a kiss on the
          floor of the Temple of Demeter on Naxos.
        </p>
        <p>
          When LiDAR on the iPhone Pro was brand new, he built the AR engine for{' '}
          <a href="https://the3dapp.com" rel="noopener">The3dApp</a>, before Matterport had anything as good on a phone.
          He grew the team from just himself to 20 in about six months, and mentored the design team on the UI. The UI
          was later redesigned; the engine experience stayed the same. The3dApp partnered with The Real Deal and Corcoran. After four internal alpha releases, and with the scope
          still growing faster than anyone could ship it, he left to travel. The company never launched and later went
          under; he and its founder are still friends.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before that came a run of startups he co-founded or built from the first line. At Renge, as CTO and co-founder, he
          designed, specified and built a location-based social app for meeting people wherever you are. Renge sponsored a
          Grammy Week party at the Roxy on the Sunset Strip, where Larry King received a lifetime achievement award. After
          that the marketing fell through, COVID emptied the places people met, and Renge ran out of users and funding.
          Its last idea was to let members send each other real toilet paper when the shelves were bare: a genuine help
          in hard times, with a smile attached. Apple rejected anything that could read as a joke about COVID, so it never
          shipped. At InnerJam, also
          as CTO and co-founder, he customised low-level C++ audio processing to tune each affirmation, wave by wave, so it
          played unheard under the user’s own music. InnerJam was completed, then sadly stopped during COVID, when the
          co-founder whose idea it was passed away. At <a href="/archive/lot-club">Lot Club</a>, as CEO and sole creator, he
          built the prototype, the website and the marketing for a private community for entertainment professionals, on a
          graph network like Renge’s, but far more powerful and on completely different tech.
          One cold email to 45,000 people, with no follow-ups, got 11% of them through an application that took at least
          15 minutes, with linked profiles, past, current and upcoming work, the studios behind it, and a case for why they
          belonged: 110 times the 0.1% of cold emails that typically book a meeting. With referrals, that made 6,000
          signups in the first month. Applicants included heads of TV networks, and early
          members came from Disney, Universal, Warner Bros., Sony, the mini-majors and indie studios.
          At Genius Inc. he built the full stack of a mobile education platform,
          led about ten developers overseas, and mentored its UX and IT staff.
        </p>
        <p>
          Earlier still, he founded Covalent Media Systems, back when it was a film services platform. He architected,
          prototyped and led development of its digital asset management cloud, which kept studio media secure for
          viewing and collaboration from pre-production through production, post-production, screeners and archival,
          for clients such as Disney, Fox Regency and The Weinstein Company, in the US and abroad. His technology has
          securely handled more than 700 films, including over 35 Academy Award–winning projects, from Harry Potter to
          Birdman, The Revenant and Star Wars. Some it carried from production through archival; for others it delivered
          the final cut securely, in place of DVDs, helping move the industry off discs. Covalent held
          contracts with every major studio, and with mini-majors and independents alike. Its security was proven by
          penetration tests and rated best in class in the quarterly reviews those contracts require: no breaches, no
          leaks.
        </p>
        <p>
          His first paid programming job came in his early teens, for a private tech millionaire. He started designing
          newsletters and magazines at 15, and was paid for his first design work at 16, the year he
          graduated high school two years early with a 4.0 GPA, the highest his small private school offered. He has been
          shipping software and design since 2006, when he started building websites freelance. Across
          twenty years of startup and executive work, in business development, product, architecture, iOS, full stack
          and design, he has led development teams of up to 50, reviewing every new developer’s code himself and
          mentoring them as they ramp up. He was also the first engineering hire for startup projects founded by a Forbes 400
          billionaire. He is also good at web scraping, and has written custom bots for jobs other platforms couldn’t
          support, or ran too slowly to be useful.
        </p>
        <ul class="profiles">
          {profiles.map((p) => (
            <li>
              <a href={p.href} rel="me noopener" aria-label={`${p.label}: ${p.handle}`}>
                {p.icon === 'github' && (
                  <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" fill="currentColor">
                    <path d="M8 0C3.58 0 0 3.58 0 8c0 3.54 2.29 6.53 5.47 7.59.4.07.55-.17.55-.38 0-.19-.01-.82-.01-1.49-2.01.37-2.53-.49-2.69-.94-.09-.23-.48-.94-.82-1.13-.28-.15-.68-.52-.01-.53.63-.01 1.08.58 1.23.82.72 1.21 1.87.87 2.33.66.07-.52.28-.87.51-1.07-1.78-.2-3.64-.89-3.64-3.95 0-.87.31-1.59.82-2.15-.08-.2-.36-1.02.08-2.12 0 0 .67-.21 2.2.82.64-.18 1.32-.27 2-.27.68 0 1.36.09 2 .27 1.53-1.04 2.2-.82 2.2-.82.44 1.1.16 1.92.08 2.12.51.56.82 1.27.82 2.15 0 3.07-1.87 3.75-3.65 3.95.29.25.54.73.54 1.48 0 1.07-.01 1.93-.01 2.2 0 .21.15.46.55.38A8.01 8.01 0 0 0 16 8c0-4.42-3.58-8-8-8z" />
                  </svg>
                )}
                {p.icon === 'linkedin' && (
                  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" fill="currentColor">
                    <rect x="2" y="2" width="20" height="20" rx="3" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" />
                    <rect x="6.2" y="10" width="2.4" height="7.5" />
                    <circle cx="7.4" cy="7.1" r="1.4" />
                    <path d="M11 10h2.3v1.1c.4-.7 1.3-1.3 2.6-1.3 2.2 0 2.9 1.4 2.9 3.4v4.3h-2.4v-3.9c0-1-.3-1.8-1.3-1.8s-1.7.7-1.7 1.9v3.8H11z" />
                  </svg>
                )}
                {p.icon === 'instagram' && (
                  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8">
                    <rect x="3" y="3" width="18" height="18" rx="5" />
                    <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="4" />
                    <circle cx="17.3" cy="6.7" r="0.6" fill="currentColor" stroke="none" />
                  </svg>
                )}
                <span>{p.label} <span class="handle">{p.handle}</span></span>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Career />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/contact">Get in touch</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/work">See the work</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .founder-name {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.25rem 0.75rem;
  }
  .full-name {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.35em;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 0.5em;
    letter-spacing: 0;
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }
  .two {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.5rem 4rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .two {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.3fr;
    }
  }
  .prose p {
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }
  .prose strong {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .portrait {
    display: block;
    width: 160px;
    height: 160px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    object-fit: cover;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    margin: 0.5rem 0 1.5rem;
  }
  .monogram {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 4.5rem;
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--accent);
    background: radial-gradient(circle at 30% 25%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 18%, var(--bg-raised)), var(--bg-raised) 70%);
    user-select: none;
  }
  .profiles {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }
  .profiles a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.65rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .profiles a:hover .handle {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .profiles svg {
    flex: none;
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }
  .handle {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    margin-left: 0.35rem;
  }
</style>
